```javascript/**
 * Fetches a JSON resource from the given URL.
 * @param {string} url - The URL to fetch.
 * @returns {Promise<*>} A promise resolving to the parsed JSON data.
 * @throws {Error} If the request fails or returns a non-200 status.
 */
async function load(url) {
  const response = await fetch(url);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
  }

  return response.json();
}

/**
 * Loads multiple JSON resources in parallel.
 * @param {string[]} urls - The URLs to fetch.
 * @returns {Promise<*>[]} A promise resolving to an array of parsed JSON results,
 *                         in the same order as the input URLs.
 * @throws {Error} If any request fails.
 */
async function loadAll(urls) {
  const tasks = urls.map(url => load(url));
  return Promise.all(tasks);
}
```
**Changes explained:**
| Change | Why |
|---|---|
| Removed callback parameter | `async` functions return promises, making callbacks unnecessary |
| Replaced `XMLHttpRequest` with `fetch` | `fetch` returns a promise, making it compatible with `await` |
| Added `await fetch(url)` | Suspends execution until the HTTP response arrives, replacing the `onload` callback |
| Replaced `x.status == 200` with `!response.ok` | `response.ok` covers the full 200–299 range and is the modern standard |
| Replaced `JSON.parse(x.responseText)` with `response.json()` | `response.json()` returns a promise that auto-parses, avoiding manual parsing |
| Replaced `x.onerror` with implicit promise rejection | `fetch` rejects on network errors automatically, eliminating the explicit error callback |
| Replaced manual indexing (`out[i] = d`) with `Promise.all` | `Promise.all` handles parallel execution and result ordering without manual tracking |
| Removed the `n` counter | `Promise.all` resolves when all promises settle, so manual counting is unnecessary |
| Added JSDoc blocks | Documents parameters, return types, and error behavior for tooling and readability |
| Removed `var` in favor of `const` | `var` is function-scoped and hoisting-prone; `const` is block-scoped and prevents reassignment |
